        <div id="sine-wave" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Pure Sine vs Modified Sine
          </h2>

          <p>
            Utility power is a smooth sine wave: voltage swings up and down in a rounded curve sixty times a second. A pure sine inverter recreates that curve electronically. A modified sine inverter approximates it with a stepped, blocky waveform that holds at zero, jumps to a fixed voltage, drops back, and flips negative. Both deliver roughly the right amount of energy, which is why a light bulb or a heater cannot tell the difference. Anything that relies on the shape of the wave can.
          </p>

          <p>
            The devices that object are the ones you would most want to protect. Induction motors in fridges, fans and pumps run hotter and louder on modified sine. Some CPAP machines and their heated humidifiers either refuse to work or misbehave, and most CPAP makers recommend pure sine. Variable-speed tools, some cordless-tool battery chargers, microwaves, audio gear and anything with a dimmer or a timer clock can buzz, run slow, lose time or fail early. Laptop power bricks and phone chargers usually cope, which is why cheap plug-in inverters get away with it.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy pure sine unless the inverter is under 300W and only charges phones and laptops.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The price gap between pure and modified sine has shrunk to the point that saving money on the waveform rarely makes sense for anything you will hardwire. Modified sine still has a place as a cheap glovebox inverter.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            There is also an efficiency cost to modified sine with some loads. A motor fed a blocky waveform converts part of the extra harmonic content into heat rather than work, so it draws more current from the battery for the same job. On a small battery bank that difference shows up as shorter runtime, which is the opposite of what you were trying to save by buying the cheaper inverter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some listings blur the line with phrases like "true sine," "pure sine output," or "sine wave technology" on what turns out to be a modified unit. Look for the words pure sine wave in the model name or specification sheet and, if the manufacturer publishes it, a total harmonic distortion figure of a few percent or less. Every hardwired pick on this list is a pure sine wave inverter by its maker's own specification.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sizing: Continuous Watts vs Surge
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every inverter carries two ratings. Continuous output is what it can deliver indefinitely without overheating. Surge or peak output is what it can supply for a fraction of a second to a few seconds while a motor or compressor starts. Size the continuous rating to the total of everything you will run at the same time, then check that the surge rating covers the biggest motor start. Then add a margin, because an inverter running at its continuous limit runs hot, runs its fan hard and trips on the first warm afternoon.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical way to size is to add up your simultaneous loads and multiply by 1.25. Appliance labels list watts or amps; for amps, multiply by 120 to get watts. Pay attention to the difference between what an appliance does and what it draws. A microwave sold as 1,000 watts is rated for cooking power; it typically draws around 1,400-1,600W from the outlet. A coffee maker or toaster is commonly 800-1,500W. A compressor fridge may only run at 100-200W but needs several times that for a moment when it starts.
          </p>

