          <p>
            The figures above are typical ranges for common appliances, not measurements of particular models, so check your own labels. They make the shape of the decision clear, though. A 1000W inverter covers everything up to a fridge, a 2,000W unit adds kitchen appliances and most saws, and 3,000W is mainly about running a couple of heavy loads together or starting stubborn motors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bigger is not free. A larger inverter idles at a higher current, costs more, weighs more, and demands thicker cable and a larger fuse. If you only ever run a laptop and a CPAP, a 2,000W inverter wastes battery every hour it sits switched on. Match the inverter to the loads you actually own rather than the ones you might someday plug in.
          </p>
