        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most people adding AC power to a van, camper or small RV, the Renogy 1000W pure sine inverter is the right size and the right waveform. Step up to a 2,000W unit like the Go Power! GP-SW2000 or GoWISE Power 2000W only when you need a microwave or coffee maker, and to the AIMS Power 3000W only for a work truck with its own battery bank.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you buy, the inverter is the easy part. Wire it with the cable and fuse its manual specifies, keep it off the starting battery for anything big, and never connect it to house wiring without a transfer switch. If you do not already have a battery bank, a portable power station is often the simpler answer.
          </p>
